[1] CP trade-off: block [[2D2, -1/2],[-1/2, D0]] PSD  <=>  P_J D0 >= 1/4  (P_J = 2 D2)
    random check of equivalence: True
    measurement-feedback check: monitoring A with Lindblad kappa gives record noise 1/(4 kappa); feedback noise P_J = 1/(4 D0): saturates
[2] reduced matter kernel K(k) = D0 + (4πG)^2 |g|^2 P_J /(hbar^2 k^4) >= (4πG/hbar)|g|^2/k^2  (DP kernel, R0 = ℓ)
    min K/floor over 5000 random (k,|g|,D0) at saturation: 1.000000 (>= 1, equality at D0 = 2πG|g|^2/(hbar k^2))
[3] g~ = 1 (exact local Newton), point particle: momentum-diffusion rate >= 4πG hbar m^2 ∫ d^3k/(2π)^3  -> cutoff^3 divergence
    electron, k-cutoff 1e+10 1/m: d<p^2>/dt >= 1.239e-75 kg^2 m^2 s^-3 ; heating 6.803e-46 W
    electron, k-cutoff 1e+15 1/m: d<p^2>/dt >= 1.239e-60 kg^2 m^2 s^-3 ; heating 6.803e-31 W
    electron, k-cutoff 1e+20 1/m: d<p^2>/dt >= 1.239e-45 kg^2 m^2 s^-3 ; heating 6.803e-16 W
    electron, k-cutoff 1e+30 1/m: d<p^2>/dt >= 1.239e-15 kg^2 m^2 s^-3 ; heating 6.803e+14 W
    -> no cutoff-free homogeneous kernel gives finite heating of a point mass (proved given the AM-GM floor)
[4] inputs: M_Cs=2.2069e-25 kg, Delta=1.90e-06 m, Gamma_max=1.429e-02 /s; 25kDa M=4.151e-23, Gamma_max=1.429e+02; LPF S_a(per mass)=1.514e-30; Earth h=7.870e-12 W/kg
[5] kernel-independent lower bounds on the smearing length ℓ (any homogeneous continuous kernel)
    (a) radiation: matter kernel >= DP(R0=ℓ) pointwise  =>  ℓ >= R0_DP_min = 4.0e-10 m (Figurato 2024 abstract; Donadi 2021: 5.40e-11)
    (b) Earth heat: per-kg heating >= G hbar/(4 sqrt(pi) ℓ^3)  =>  ℓ >= 5.02e-12 m
    (c) LPF force-noise floor S_a >= (8π/3)G hbar ∫|g|^2 S_T /M^2  =>  ℓ >= 5.27e-14 m
        floor at ℓ=1e-15: 3.97e-27 ; at 4e-10: 6.06e-40 ; at 1e-5: 6.05e-40  (LPF per mass 1.51e-30)
    (d) stated bounds only, Cs(1.9um,70s) x LPF, Cauchy-Schwarz over all kernels: excluded iff ℓ < 1.00e-19 m
        ratio (needed/available) at ℓ=1e-15: 1.40e-07 ; 1e-13: 2.86e-13
        same with OSSW-2022 torsion bar (sigma_a=1e-7): ratio at 1e-15: 1.06e-23
    (e) upper end: ISL tested to 52 um (Lee et al. 2020, comparator); Gaussian smearing changes the two-body potential by erfc(r/2ℓ):
        ℓ=5.0e-06: erfc(52um/2ℓ) = 1.92e-13
        ℓ=1.0e-05: erfc(52um/2ℓ) = 2.36e-04
        ℓ=1.5e-05: erfc(52um/2ℓ) = 1.42e-02
        ℓ=2.0e-05: erfc(52um/2ℓ) = 6.60e-02
